 - **`ps_import_run` shape: which fields stay standalone columns vs move into the `options` blob** — raised in round 8, to settle when writing the PR2 schema (criterion, candidate list and the knock-on changes to `ImportRunContext`/`ImportRunOptions` are in the schema-deltas section above).
-- **Coalescing `ImportMessage` by row ids** (raised in round 9). The quiet caches keep each ambiguity/creation message to once per BATCH, but a run split into N batches still repeats it N times, and per-row messages (an invalid date on 4 000 rows) repeat regardless. Sketch: messages are equal when every field except the row matches, and the row becomes a cumulative list, so the report shows one line carrying many row numbers. **Deferred to PR2 on purpose**: the merge belongs where messages are persisted and capped — the same code that owns the `messages` JSON, the 1 000-per-severity cap and `message_counts` — and PR1 has no consumer that would benefit (the test harness just concatenates). The one piece worth settling early is the DTO shape, since `ImportMessage::$row` becoming `$rows` (or gaining a count) is cheaper to decide before PR2 writes the schema than after.
-- **Pre-announcing auto-creations during validation** (raised in round 9). The database-phase notices (decision 27) tell the merchant what was created, but only after the fact. Announcing it in the PAUSING validation phase instead would turn it into a real gate — "this file will create 12 categories and 3 brands, continue?" — which is the version worth having once the BO modal exists. **Not built now**, and when it is: it must reuse the resolvers in a READ-ONLY mode rather than re-implement the lookups, because a second copy of the category walk would have to agree with the real one, which is exactly the trap round 8 removed by giving both accessory phases one shared classifier. Note the cascade makes it tractable without resolving parents that do not exist yet: once a path segment misses, every segment below it is necessarily new, so the preview can count creations by walking until the first miss.
